## Draft, complete and enabled are different

A saved quiet-hours draft is not a working notification rule. Mark a task complete by a clear product rule, or ask the person to say they have finished when a human check is needed. Enabling the result is a separate, named action. When people return, show their saved work, the dependency state and any devices that changed.

<%= example('nhs-tasks-resume-draft') %>

## Repair without losing progress

Show a problem only when there is something to fix. “Sensor unavailable” does not mean the person answered wrongly. Tell a temporarily unavailable integration apart from a removed selection, keep the unaffected answers and say whether the person can continue.

<%= example('nhs-tasks-repair') %>

## Write and build it accessibly

- Name tasks as household activities: “Choose household events”, not “Configure trigger schema”.
- Tie each task link to its text status, with the name before the status in reading order.
- Group a long setup under headings.
- Do not rely on a coloured tag, a tick or a percentage alone.
- Let rows wrap at small widths.
- If completion depends on notes or a talk with someone else, ask whether the task is finished. Do not guess durations or show countdowns.
- Keep “progress saved” messages apart from device notifications, so the page does not announce every background update.

Test with the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html).

## Keep the boundary clear

Save draft answers and completion deliberately. Do not infer a saved draft from a partly drawn screen. Check capability-dependent tasks again when integrations change. A task link must open a real, supported configuration route.
